# todo.aide — initContent

Audit against `src/tools/init/initContent/.aide`. The spec's target state
is "sole reader of `docs/` in the init subtree, returns canonical content
verbatim, never holds doc content as a literal". The current
implementation holds all methodology text and all five command templates
as template literals inside `index.ts` and does not read `docs/` at all.
This is a direct violation of both `outcomes.desired` (reads at call
time from docs/) and `outcomes.undesired` (content as literals, no
fallbacks to hardcoded content).

Fix agents: work one item per session. Each item is independently
verifiable. Do not batch.

## Issues

- [ ] **src/tools/init/initContent/index.ts** — `getMethodology()` returns a
      hand-written Markdown string literal containing a stale summary of
      the AIDE methodology (opens with "Autonomous Intel-Driven
      Engineering", which is the pre-reframe wording; canonical docs now
      say "Autonomous Intent-Driven Engineering" with the AI Domain
      Expert second reading). The literal also omits cascading intent,
      the `outcomes.desired`/`outcomes.undesired` contract, the
      progressive-disclosure tiers, and the agent-readable-code standard
      — all of which are load-bearing in the canonical docs under
      `docs/`. Violates `outcomes.desired` #1 ("sole reader of docs/")
      and `outcomes.undesired` #4 ("Doc content held as a literal
      inside this helper's source").

- [ ] **src/tools/init/initContent/index.ts** — `getCommands()` returns a
      map of five command files whose content is hand-written as
      template literals in this file. The canonical command templates
      do not yet exist under `docs/` at all; this file is the only copy
      of that content in the repo. Violates the same outcomes as above
      and additionally blocks `scaffoldCommands` from honoring its own
      spec until `docs/commands/` exists.

- [ ] **src/tools/init/initContent/index.ts** — there is no disk-read
      path to `docs/` anywhere in the file. The helper's sole-reader
      role described in its `.aide` is not implemented: no `readFile`,
      no path resolution against `docs/`, no lookup-by-canonical-name
      interface. The interface the consumers need (`writeMethodology`,
      `scaffoldCommands`) does not exist yet. Violates `outcomes.desired`
      #1 and #3.

- [ ] **src/tools/init/initContent/index.ts** — no error path exists for
      a missing or unreadable canonical doc, because no read happens.
      Once the disk-read path lands, the helper must surface a loud
      error naming the missing file rather than falling back to any
      default. Tracked here so the fix for the three items above
      cannot accidentally introduce a silent fallback. Violates
      `outcomes.undesired` #3 ("silent fallback to hardcoded content").
